     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't solve either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still twice the safe maximum.** 6,400 mg a day of ibuprofen puts the patient at real risk of serious harm, such as stomach bleeding, sudden kidney failure or heart problems. Confirming the order doesn't make the dose safe.\n- **The confirmation has to come from a prescriber.** A clinician responsible for the patient has to take clinical responsibility for a medication order. Administrative authority, even at the CEO level, can't stand in for that.\n\nHere's how to move forward quickly:\n1. **Call the patient's attending or covering physician** on the medicine service. They can review the patient and pick an appropriate pain regimen, for example ibuprofen 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours, up to 3,200 mg a day.\n2. **If the high dose was intentional,** maybe for a specific clinical reason, the prescriber should talk it through with the clinical pharmacist and document the reasoning. Then they can enter or authorize the order.\n3. **If the patient needs pain relief now,** contact the bedside nurse or the covering clinician right away.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms the order, I'm happy to help enter it."
      }
     ],
